# Template Strings vs Plain Context Paths in Fluwy YAML

In Fluwy YAML files, there are two ways to reference context values. Using the wrong one causes bugs.

## Plain context paths (no `${}`) -- for objects, arrays, and data references

Use plain paths when a property expects the **actual value** (object, array, boolean, number). Plain paths resolve to the real JavaScript value.

```yaml
# CORRECT -- passes the actual array
table:
  data: contacts

# CORRECT -- passes the actual object
post:
  data: form.data

# CORRECT -- passes the actual value
vars:
  contact: response.data
```

## Template strings (`${...}`) -- for inserting values into text

Use `${...}` when embedding a value inside a **display string** like text content, URLs, or messages. Internally this uses `String.replace()`, which calls `.toString()` on resolved values -- so objects/arrays become `[object Object]`.

```yaml
# CORRECT -- inserting into text
h1: Hello, ${user.name}!
get: /api/contacts/${params.id}
notify: "Saved ${contact.name}"

# WRONG -- converts array to "[object Object]"
table:
  data: ${contacts}
```

## Quick Rule

- Properties expecting **data structures** (like `data`, `vars` values, `path`): use plain paths
- Properties expecting **display text** (like `content`, `text`, `title`, URLs): use `${...}`
